# Laboratory Patient Flow

Lab owns sample-to-result execution for encounter-linked orders; keep the lab workbench focused on receiving orders, collecting samples, processing tests, and releasing verified results. Lab may also place walk-in orders by creating patients and Lab encounters from the create-lab-request flow.

## Stage Sequence

- Order received from OPD, IPD, ICU, clinical dialog, or Lab walk-in create.
- Billing gate checked when required.
- Sample collected, labeled, and received or rejected.
- Test processed and results entered.
- Supervisor verifies or releases the result.
- Critical results notify the receiving clinician (when a clinical encounter owns the order).

## Handoffs

- Doctor or clinical user owns clinical order placement on OPD/IPD/ICU encounters.
- Lab tech owns walk-in order create (patient + Lab encounter when needed), collection, accession, processing, and result entry.
- Lab supervisor owns verification and release.
- OPD or IPD orchestrators own stage progression after results return on clinical encounters.

## Hard Invariants

- Must not advance OPD/IPD stages directly; may signal diagnostics sync for clinical encounters only.
- Must not duplicate orders already posted through the encounter.
- Must keep every order tied to an encounter id (clinical or Lab walk-in).
- When creating encounters from Lab, use `encounter_type` `LAB` only—not OPD/IPD/EMERGENCY/THEATRE.
- Must not hide critical results from the clinician workflow when a clinical recipient exists.

## Allowed Critical Actions

- Use the workbench route as the canonical lab workspace.
- Create lab orders for existing patients; create or reuse an open Lab encounter when none is selected.
- Create patients through the create-lab-request flow when `patient:write` is granted.
- May support pay-now or bill-later at order time when policy requires it.
- Flag critical results immediately for notification.
- Keep OPD and IPD order contexts distinct in the queue; Lab walk-in rows remain Lab-encounter scoped.

